WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:03.300
-We are here with
James Beard Award-winning chef

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:05.634
Rick Martínez,
whose book -- whose cookbook,

00:00:05.634 --> 00:00:08.300
"Mi Cocina: Recipes and Rapture
From My Kitchen in Mexico,"

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:09.334
is available now.

00:00:09.334 --> 00:00:10.601
Rick, what are we
going to make today?

00:00:10.601 --> 00:00:12.467
Thank you for being here.
-Thanks for having me.

00:00:12.467 --> 00:00:14.501
Well, it's the holidays.
-Yes.

00:00:14.501 --> 00:00:15.601
-So we have to make tamales.

00:00:15.601 --> 00:00:17.968
-Great.
-But first we have a cocktail.

00:00:17.968 --> 00:00:19.701
-This is the cocktail here.
-This is the cocktail, yes.

00:00:19.701 --> 00:00:21.367
-Oh, it's very warm.
It has a warm feeling to it.

00:00:21.367 --> 00:00:22.467
-Yes, I know.

00:00:22.467 --> 00:00:24.167
So, I'm going to let you
pour the tequila.

00:00:24.167 --> 00:00:25.734
-Oh, there's --
Tequila goes in this.

00:00:25.734 --> 00:00:27.067
-Yes.
-Is this...

00:00:27.067 --> 00:00:28.667
This isn't now mulled wine,
is it, right?

00:00:28.667 --> 00:00:32.534
-No, this --
this is a hibiscus hot toddy.

00:00:32.534 --> 00:00:34.467
-Oh! I'm very excited.

00:00:34.467 --> 00:00:37.133
-So we've got some
cinnamon, peppercorn, clove,

00:00:37.133 --> 00:00:38.300
and then, obviously tequila.

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:39.567
-Alright. Thank you.
Here we go. Cheers.

00:00:39.567 --> 00:00:40.534
Happy holidays, everybody.

00:00:40.534 --> 00:00:43.534
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:43.534 --> 00:00:45.167
No, this is wonderful.

00:00:45.167 --> 00:00:46.434
I've had tamales before.

00:00:46.434 --> 00:00:49.133
I've never had a brown butter
pecan tamale.

00:00:49.133 --> 00:00:51.100
Is this like a dessert --
a dessert tamale?

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:52.601
-It is a complete dessert.
I'm going to switch places.

00:00:52.601 --> 00:00:53.567
-Okay. Great.
-Real quick.

00:00:53.567 --> 00:00:55.267
Um, so, yeah, I love tamales.

00:00:55.267 --> 00:00:58.000
And not a lot of Americans
or Mexicans actually know

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:00.400
that you can make sweet tamales.

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:03.501
And this is kind of inspired
by my love of

00:01:03.501 --> 00:01:05.234
butter pecan ice cream.
-Okay, great.

00:01:05.234 --> 00:01:08.767
-So toasted pecans, brown
butter, and masa.

00:01:08.767 --> 00:01:11.000
-Alright. Great. And so how do
you go about it here?

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:13.801
-Okay.
So I'm going to have you stir.

00:01:13.801 --> 00:01:15.200
-Stir? Okay. Great.

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:17.601
-This is the masa harina
and the toasted pecans.

00:01:17.601 --> 00:01:18.868
And we have very --
-I'm doing very good so far.

00:01:18.868 --> 00:01:21.167
-Yes, we have half and half.
-Okay.

00:01:21.167 --> 00:01:23.501
-And sweetened condensed milk.
-Great.

00:01:23.501 --> 00:01:25.000
-Some vanilla.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:27.434
We're gonna, like, play up those
kind of ice creamy flavors.

00:01:27.434 --> 00:01:30.434
And then to play up
the toasted nuts,

00:01:30.434 --> 00:01:31.701
we've got some brown butter

00:01:31.701 --> 00:01:33.100
because also I
love brown butter.

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:35.167
-Alright.
This is very good, very light.

00:01:35.167 --> 00:01:37.367
-Yeah, doesn't it seem light?
-It does seem light.

00:01:37.367 --> 00:01:38.634
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-It does. Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:01:38.634 --> 00:01:40.300
-Okay. So, yeah, really, really,
really get in there.

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:41.367
-Gotcha.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:01:41.367 --> 00:01:42.467
-But I could really,
really do this,

00:01:42.467 --> 00:01:43.767
but I can also
tell someone already has,

00:01:43.767 --> 00:01:44.701
so why don't we skip
to that part?

00:01:44.701 --> 00:01:46.300
-Okay.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:47.534
-You know,
who are we doing here, right?

00:01:47.534 --> 00:01:50.367
It's not CGI.
They all can see it.

00:01:50.367 --> 00:01:52.234
-Okay. So, now
we're going to roll them up.

00:01:52.234 --> 00:01:53.667
So I'll do one for you.

00:01:53.667 --> 00:01:55.000
Grab grab a...
-Alright. Great.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:58.434
And while we roll,
I want to ask about the book

00:01:58.434 --> 00:01:59.868
because one of
the things you did,

00:01:59.868 --> 00:02:01.934
I think the biggest mistake
anybody could make is saying,

00:02:01.934 --> 00:02:03.367
"Oh, Mexican food
is one thing."

00:02:03.367 --> 00:02:06.501
You went to all
the different regions and --

00:02:06.501 --> 00:02:07.868
and was that
a fun journey for you?

00:02:07.868 --> 00:02:09.501
Did you know it all,
or did you learn stuff?

00:02:09.501 --> 00:02:11.734
-No. I grew up in Texas,
and I grew up

00:02:11.734 --> 00:02:12.734
thinking that --
-Whoo!

00:02:12.734 --> 00:02:13.667
-Whoo-hoo!

00:02:13.667 --> 00:02:15.367
[ Chuckles ] Hook 'em.
-Yeah.

00:02:15.367 --> 00:02:18.067
-So I grew up thinking
that we ate Mexican food,

00:02:18.067 --> 00:02:21.234
you know, like, and really,
it was Tex-Mex and, um...

00:02:21.234 --> 00:02:22.801
Sorry. Okay.
-No, no. This is --

00:02:22.801 --> 00:02:24.501
What I'm doing is good.
-Yeah, yeah. And then just --

00:02:24.501 --> 00:02:26.367
[ Laughs ]
It's perfect. It's beautiful.

00:02:26.367 --> 00:02:27.467
Exactly.
-Oh, my God. Look at that.

00:02:27.467 --> 00:02:29.834
[ Stammering ]
-There you go.

00:02:29.834 --> 00:02:33.467
Um, I grew up thinking that
we ate Mexican food, and so I...

00:02:33.467 --> 00:02:35.400
When I started
traveling in Mexico,

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:38.033
I was like, "This is nothing
like what we ate."

00:02:38.033 --> 00:02:39.367
-Right.
-And so I just wanted

00:02:39.367 --> 00:02:42.267
to explore, and so I bought
a car in Mexico City

00:02:42.267 --> 00:02:44.868
and literally drove
about 30,000 miles

00:02:44.868 --> 00:02:47.534
exploring the entire country,
every state.

00:02:47.534 --> 00:02:50.100
-That's amazing.
-Yeah. Eating my way around.

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:52.968
-And now tell me, there's a --
I think a lot of times

00:02:52.968 --> 00:02:55.100
people use
the word authentic, uh,

00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:57.167
to refer to foods
from different countries.

00:02:57.167 --> 00:02:58.601
You do not care
for the word authentic.

00:02:58.601 --> 00:03:02.701
-I actually hate that word,
because I feel like,

00:03:02.701 --> 00:03:04.100
like in this country,

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:07.367
Mexican cooks
cook with what they love,

00:03:07.367 --> 00:03:09.801
with what they have,
with what's in season.

00:03:09.801 --> 00:03:13.300
And so here we talk about
authentic as being like,

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:15.667
this is the one way
that you make tamales.

00:03:15.667 --> 00:03:18.667
And so I started exploring
the country of Mexico

00:03:18.667 --> 00:03:21.267
and thinking I'm going to find
that one version of pozole,

00:03:21.267 --> 00:03:23.367
that one version
of the tamale,

00:03:23.367 --> 00:03:27.334
and actually, no,
everybody just cooks for fun.

00:03:27.334 --> 00:03:29.501
It's their creative outlet.

00:03:29.501 --> 00:03:31.067
If you invite 10 people
over to your house

00:03:31.067 --> 00:03:32.400
and you ask them
to make the same dish,

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:33.667
you're going to have 10

00:03:33.667 --> 00:03:36.100
amazingly different
versions of it.

00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:38.634
And so I feel like, you know,

00:03:38.634 --> 00:03:40.334
we shouldn't -- we don't
need that word.

00:03:40.334 --> 00:03:42.367
-Yeah. Give yourself
the freedom to explore outside

00:03:42.367 --> 00:03:43.467
of whatever authentic is.

00:03:43.467 --> 00:03:44.834
Alright.
Now show me what we do here.

00:03:44.834 --> 00:03:47.033
-Okay.
-Very excited.

00:03:47.033 --> 00:03:49.467
-So this is actually
my favorite moment.

00:03:49.467 --> 00:03:51.834
This is the moment when you've
been waiting for 45 minutes.

00:03:51.834 --> 00:03:53.100
We've steamed these tamales

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:56.567
and now we have the reveal.
-Oh, my goodness.

00:03:56.567 --> 00:04:00.167
-Yes.
-Oh. Yes.

00:04:00.167 --> 00:04:01.501
-Oh, they really smell good.
Alright.

00:04:01.501 --> 00:04:04.968
-Yeah, yeah. So -- But...
-They're so hot.

00:04:04.968 --> 00:04:06.133
-I actually have...

00:04:06.133 --> 00:04:07.734
-Why did I stick
my hand in there?

00:04:07.734 --> 00:04:09.467
-Because it's fun.
-Okay, great. Oh, see?

00:04:09.467 --> 00:04:10.567
-For you.
-That's when you got to go,

00:04:10.567 --> 00:04:12.467
"Stop, stop,
we made it already."

00:04:12.467 --> 00:04:14.100
-Exactly.
-Okay, great.

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:15.968
-And to gild the lily
a little bit,

00:04:15.968 --> 00:04:19.234
I added some dulce de leche
and some extra toasted pecans.

00:04:19.234 --> 00:04:21.834
-This looks fantastic.
I'm very excited, Rick.

00:04:21.834 --> 00:04:23.334
-Alright. Cheers.

00:04:23.334 --> 00:04:24.968
[ Laughter ]

00:04:24.968 --> 00:04:26.200
Oh, well. Yeah, yeah.

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:28.501
[ Laughs ]
-Mmm!

00:04:28.501 --> 00:04:30.801
This is amazing, everybody!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:30.801 --> 00:04:33.868
Give it up
for Chef Rick Martínez!

00:04:33.868 --> 00:04:37.334
"Mi Cocina: Recipes and Rapture
From My Kitchen in Mexico"

00:04:37.334 --> 00:04:38.534
is available now.

00:04:38.534 --> 00:04:39.634
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

